---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
---

<Layout
  title="Policy testing — The Relic"
  description="Test every policy rule against your real agent history before you ship it."
>
  <div class="min-h-screen">
    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="border-b border-white/5">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-6">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between">
          <a href="/" class="text-xl font-bold text-white">The Relic</a>
          <nav class="flex items-center gap-6">
            <a href="/" class="text-sm font-medium text-neutral-400 hover:text-white">Home</a>
            <a href="/policy-testing" class="text-sm font-medium text-relic-400">Policy testing</a>
            <a href="/self-host" class="text-sm font-medium text-neutral-400 hover:text-white">Self-host</a>
            <a href="https://github.com/therelicai/therelic" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-sm font-medium text-neutral-400 hover:text-white">GitHub</a>
          </nav>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Hero -->
    <section class="py-16 sm:py-24">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6">
        <p class="text-sm font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-relic-400">Replay & diff</p>
        <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl">
          Test every rule against your real history before you ship it.
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-neutral-400">
          Edit a policy in the console. Before you save, The Relic replays the last 1, 7,
          or 30 days of your agent's actual decisions against the candidate policy and
          tells you exactly which verdicts would have changed — with sample run IDs you
          can click into. Policy reviews stop being a paper exercise.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- The trust loop -->
    <section class="border-t border-white/5 py-16 sm:py-24">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">The trust loop</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 max-w-2xl text-neutral-400">
          Three steps, no infrastructure to add. The same engine that enforces policy
          on the agent's host is the engine that scores the diff — there is no second
          implementation that could drift from production.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-6">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-relic-400">1</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-white">Edit a rule</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-400">
              Open the policy editor, write the rule you're considering. Nothing is
              saved yet — your fleet is still running the old policy.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-6">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-relic-400">2</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-white">Replay against history</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-400">
              Pick a window (1d / 7d / 30d). The Relic re-evaluates every recorded
              action under the candidate policy and reports the deltas — usually in
              well under ten seconds.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-6">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-relic-400">3</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-white">Inspect the flips</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-400">
              Click the diff badge. See up to five representative flipped runs in each
              direction, with the original verdict and the new one side by side. If
              the samples look unintended, you find out before you ship.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- What the badge tells you -->
    <section class="border-t border-white/5 py-16 sm:py-24">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">What the badge tells you</h2>
        <div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-3">
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-6">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-relic-400">Would-deny</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-white">Actions the new rule would have blocked</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-400">
              The headline number. If you're tightening a policy, this is the count of
              previously-allowed actions that would now fail. Useful runs are linked.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-6">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-relic-400">Would-allow</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-white">Actions the rule would have unblocked</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-400">
              For broadening changes. Tells you whether the new rule lets through
              denials that were previously caught — including ones you might not have
              intended to allow.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/40 p-6">
            <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-relic-400">Unchanged</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-white">Verdicts that stay the same</p>
            <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-neutral-400">
              The reassuring number. Zero flips means the rule edit was a no-op
              against the recorded fleet — useful when refactoring without intending
              behavior change.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Engineering note -->
    <section class="border-t border-white/5 py-16 sm:py-24">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white">Engineering note</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-neutral-400">
          The simulator runs the exact same policy evaluator the runtime uses to
          enforce on the agent's host — the platform vendors the engine source from
          the runtime and a CI job refuses any drift. The diff badge can't lie about
          what production would do, because production runs the same code path.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 text-neutral-400">
          Traces stay where you keep them. If you self-host, the replay job runs
          against your own Postgres + S3 — your action history never leaves your
          infrastructure.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Footer link back -->
    <section class="border-t border-white/5 py-12">
      <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6">
        <a href="/" class="text-sm text-neutral-400 hover:text-white">← Back to home</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
